<p>
There&rsquo;s a tremendous amount of excitement about Apple&rsquo;s new foldable iPhone, the Duo. A grown journalist squealed like a teenager when she saw John Ternus sporting one at a recent event. Apple is claiming that demand is so high that fulfillment wait times on orders could be as long as 3 months.
</p>
<p>
I hate to be the guy raining on everyone&rsquo;s parade but I don&rsquo;t get it. Rumors of an Apple foldable phone have been around for years but I always hoped they weren&rsquo;t true. I&rsquo;d much rather Apple devote its time to improving the regular iPhone or perhaps finally taking HomeKit seriously. The results from other manufacturers where not encouraging. All their foldable offerings were a disaster, some barely surviving their unboxing. Apple says they&rsquo;ve solved all those problems but this thing has a foldable screen and it&rsquo;s hard to believe it&rsquo;s not going to have problems with all those repeated foldings and unfoldings.
</p>
<p>
Regardless, why would you want a folding phone? I just don&rsquo;t see the attraction. John Gruber <a href="https://daringfireball.net/2026/10/the_iphone_duo">suggests an answer</a>. The Duo, he says, is a terrible phone, far worse than any phone Apple has ever made. But he notes that what it does have going for it is that it&rsquo;s a great tablet that folds up and fits in your pocket. If that&rsquo;s what you&rsquo;re looking for, maybe the Duo is right for you. If what you want is a phone, any recent iPhone is a much better bet. I agree with all that but I can&rsquo;t get over the idea that the Duo is just a stunt.</p>
